__d(function(e,i,t,a,n,s,o){n.exports={version:"2.0",board:"Karnataka State Board / NCERT",grade:7,subject:"Mathematics",textbook:"Mathematics \u2014 Textbook for Class VII",chapter_number:1,chapter_title:"Integers",estimated_study_time_minutes:60,difficulty:"Easy",learning_objectives:["Recall and extend understanding of integers from Class VI","Perform addition and subtraction of integers on a number line","Apply properties of addition of integers: closure, commutative, associative, additive identity","Multiply integers and understand the rules of signs in multiplication","Apply properties of multiplication of integers: closure, commutative, associative, distributive, multiplicative identity","Divide integers and understand division rules including division by zero","Solve word problems involving all four operations on integers","Understand that integers are used in real-life contexts such as temperature, sea level, and debt"],prerequisite_knowledge:["Understanding of natural numbers and whole numbers from earlier classes","Basic concept of negative numbers and the number line from Class VI","Addition and subtraction of whole numbers","Multiplication tables and basic division"],introduction:{why_important:"Integers extend our number system to include negative numbers, making it possible to represent real-world quantities like temperatures below zero, depths below sea level, losses in business, and floors below ground level. Without integers, mathematics cannot describe the real world fully.",real_life_relevance:"Integers appear everywhere: the temperature in Leh, Ladakh in January can be \u221215\xb0C; a business loss of \u20b95,000 is written as \u22125000; the Mariana Trench is at \u221211,034 m; a lift going 3 floors below ground is at \u22123. Bank accounts, cricket scores (run rate vs required rate), and weather forecasts all use integers.",karnataka_context:"In Karnataka, sea level measurements use 0 as the reference \u2014 the coast of Mangaluru is at sea level (0 m), while Bengaluru is at +921 m. The temperature in Bengaluru rarely goes negative, but in Kodagu's high hills it can touch 5\u20138\xb0C in winter. Stock market movements (positive = gain, negative = loss) are integers that affect Karnataka's businesses every day.",connection_to_previous_learning:"In Class VI, students were introduced to negative numbers and integers on the number line. Class VII extends this to all four operations (addition, subtraction, multiplication, division) with integers and their properties."},story_summary:{characters:[{name:"The Number Line",role:"The visual tool that makes integer operations intuitive \u2014 moving right for addition/positive, left for subtraction/negative"},{name:"Properties of Operations",role:"Closure, Commutative, Associative, Distributive \u2014 the rules that govern how integers behave under operations"},{name:"Signs in Multiplication/Division",role:"The rule that determines the sign of a product or quotient: same signs = positive, different signs = negative"}],plot:["The chapter opens by revisiting integers on the number line \u2014 positive integers to the right, negative to the left, zero in the middle.","It develops addition of integers using the number line: adding a positive means moving right; adding a negative means moving left.","It introduces properties of addition: closure (sum of two integers is always an integer), commutative (a+b = b+a), associative ((a+b)+c = a+(b+c)), additive identity (a+0 = a), additive inverse (a + (\u2212a) = 0).","It explores multiplication of integers, establishing the sign rules through patterns: positive \xd7 positive = positive, positive \xd7 negative = negative, negative \xd7 negative = positive.","It develops properties of multiplication: closure, commutative, associative, distributive over addition, multiplicative identity (1), and the role of zero (any integer \xd7 0 = 0).","It introduces division of integers with sign rules mirroring multiplication, and the important rule that division by zero is undefined.","The chapter closes with word problems applying all operations to real-world contexts."],moral:"Integers are a complete and consistent number system \u2014 their operations follow clear, predictable rules (properties) that make calculation reliable. Negative numbers are not 'bad' numbers \u2014 they are essential tools for describing a world that has direction and opposition."},core_concepts:[{concept_name:"Addition and Subtraction of Integers",simple_explanation:"To add integers, use the number line \u2014 move right for positive, left for negative. Subtracting an integer is the same as adding its opposite: a \u2212 b = a + (\u2212b).",detailed_explanation:"On the number line, start at the first integer. To add a positive integer, move right by that many steps. To add a negative integer, move left by that many steps. For subtraction: a \u2212 b = a + (\u2212b). So 5 \u2212 (\u22123) = 5 + 3 = 8; and \u22124 \u2212 3 = \u22124 + (\u22123) = \u22127. Properties of addition: (1) Closure: a + b is always an integer. (2) Commutative: a + b = b + a. (3) Associative: (a + b) + c = a + (b + c). (4) Additive Identity: a + 0 = a. (5) Additive Inverse: a + (\u2212a) = 0.",real_life_example:"Temperature: In the morning the temperature is \u22123\xb0C and it rises by 7\xb0C. New temperature = \u22123 + 7 = 4\xb0C. Or: a submarine is at \u221250 m and dives 30 m more: \u221250 + (\u221230) = \u221280 m.",karnataka_example:"Bengaluru's altitude is +921 m. The Dead Sea is at \u2212430 m. Difference in altitude = 921 \u2212 (\u2212430) = 921 + 430 = 1351 m. This subtraction of a negative integer is the same as addition.",common_mistakes:"Students often confuse a \u2212 (\u2212b) with a \u2212 b. Remember: subtracting a negative is adding a positive: 5 \u2212 (\u22123) = 5 + 3 = 8 (NOT 5 \u2212 3 = 2). Also, the sign of the result when adding integers depends on which number has the larger absolute value \u2014 e.g., \u22128 + 3 = \u22125, not +5.",memory_tip:"NUMBER LINE RULE: Right = positive, Left = negative. Subtracting = reverse direction. 'Minus a minus = plus': a \u2212 (\u2212b) = a + b. Two negatives cancel each other out."},{concept_name:"Multiplication of Integers",simple_explanation:"Multiplying two integers: same signs give positive, different signs give negative. (+)(+) = +, (\u2212)(\u2212) = +, (+)(\u2212) = \u2212, (\u2212)(+) = \u2212.",detailed_explanation:"Sign rules for multiplication: Positive \xd7 Positive = Positive (e.g., 3 \xd7 4 = 12). Positive \xd7 Negative = Negative (e.g., 3 \xd7 (\u22124) = \u221212). Negative \xd7 Positive = Negative (e.g., (\u22123) \xd7 4 = \u221212). Negative \xd7 Negative = Positive (e.g., (\u22123) \xd7 (\u22124) = 12). The product of an even number of negative integers is positive; an odd number of negative integers gives a negative product. Properties: (1) Closure: product of two integers is an integer. (2) Commutative: a \xd7 b = b \xd7 a. (3) Associative: (a \xd7 b) \xd7 c = a \xd7 (b \xd7 c). (4) Distributive: a \xd7 (b + c) = a\xd7b + a\xd7c. (5) Multiplicative Identity: a \xd7 1 = a. (6) Zero property: a \xd7 0 = 0.",real_life_example:"A person loses \u20b9200 per day. After 5 days, change in money = \u2212200 \xd7 5 = \u2212\u20b91000 (a loss of \u20b91000). If the losses are reversed (money returned), it becomes \u2212(\u2212200 \xd7 5) = +\u20b91000.",karnataka_example:"A Karnataka farmer's crop fails for 3 consecutive months, losing \u20b94,500 each month. Total loss = (\u22124500) \xd7 3 = \u2212\u20b913,500. If the government compensates double, compensation = 2 \xd7 (\u221213500) \xd7 (\u22121) = +\u20b927,000.",common_mistakes:"Students forget that negative \xd7 negative = positive. A common error: (\u22123) \xd7 (\u22124) = \u221212 instead of +12. Remember: two negatives in multiplication always give a positive. Also, negative \xd7 positive is always negative \u2014 the sign of the larger number does NOT matter in multiplication (unlike addition).",memory_tip:"SAME SIGNS = SMILEY (positive +). DIFFERENT SIGNS = SAD (negative \u2212). Or: 'Enemy of enemy = friend' \u2014 negative \xd7 negative = positive. Count negatives: EVEN number of negatives in a product = POSITIVE result."},{concept_name:"Division of Integers",simple_explanation:"Division of integers follows the same sign rules as multiplication: same signs give positive quotient, different signs give negative quotient. Division by zero is undefined (not allowed).",detailed_explanation:"Sign rules for division (identical to multiplication): Positive \xf7 Positive = Positive (e.g., 12 \xf7 4 = 3). Negative \xf7 Negative = Positive (e.g., (\u221212) \xf7 (\u22124) = 3). Positive \xf7 Negative = Negative (e.g., 12 \xf7 (\u22124) = \u22123). Negative \xf7 Positive = Negative (e.g., (\u221212) \xf7 4 = \u22123). IMPORTANT: Division by zero is undefined \u2014 you cannot divide any number by 0. Also, 0 \xf7 any non-zero integer = 0. Note: division of integers does not always give an integer result (e.g., (\u22127) \xf7 2 = \u22123.5), so integers are NOT closed under division.",real_life_example:"A group of 4 friends share a loss of \u20b91200 equally. Each person's share = (\u22121200) \xf7 4 = \u2212\u20b9300 (each loses \u20b9300). If 4 friends each owed \u20b9300, total debt = 4 \xd7 (\u2212300) = \u2212\u20b91200.",karnataka_example:"Bengaluru's temperature drops 6\xb0C over 3 days equally. Drop per day = \u22126 \xf7 3 = \u22122\xb0C per day. Or: a Bengaluru business makes a loss of \u20b936,000 over 6 months. Average monthly loss = (\u221236000) \xf7 6 = \u2212\u20b96,000 per month.",common_mistakes:"Students sometimes think 0 \xf7 0 = 1 or 0 \xf7 5 = undefined. Remember: 0 \xf7 any non-zero integer = 0 (zero divided by anything non-zero is zero). Any number \xf7 0 = undefined (you cannot divide by zero, ever). Also, integers are NOT closed under division: (\u22127) \xf7 2 = \u22123.5, which is not an integer.",memory_tip:"Division sign rules = same as multiplication sign rules. SAME signs \u2192 POSITIVE. DIFFERENT signs \u2192 NEGATIVE. And NEVER divide by ZERO (the calculator shows ERROR for a reason \u2014 it's mathematically impossible)."}],key_terms_glossary:[{term:"Integer",meaning:"Any whole number, positive, negative, or zero. The set of integers = {\u2026, \u22123, \u22122, \u22121, 0, 1, 2, 3, \u2026}. Integers do not include fractions or decimals."},{term:"Additive Inverse",meaning:"The additive inverse of an integer a is \u2212a. When added together, they give 0: a + (\u2212a) = 0. Example: additive inverse of 5 is \u22125; additive inverse of \u22127 is 7."},{term:"Additive Identity",meaning:"Zero (0) is the additive identity for integers. Adding 0 to any integer leaves it unchanged: a + 0 = a."},{term:"Multiplicative Identity",meaning:"One (1) is the multiplicative identity. Multiplying any integer by 1 leaves it unchanged: a \xd7 1 = a."},{term:"Closure Property",meaning:"A set is closed under an operation if performing that operation on any two members of the set always gives a result that is also in the set. Integers are closed under addition, subtraction, and multiplication \u2014 but NOT division."},{term:"Commutative Property",meaning:"The order of operands does not change the result. For integers: a + b = b + a (addition is commutative); a \xd7 b = b \xd7 a (multiplication is commutative). Subtraction and division are NOT commutative."},{term:"Associative Property",meaning:"The grouping of operands does not change the result. (a + b) + c = a + (b + c) for addition; (a \xd7 b) \xd7 c = a \xd7 (b \xd7 c) for multiplication. Subtraction and division are NOT associative."},{term:"Distributive Property",meaning:"Multiplication distributes over addition (and subtraction): a \xd7 (b + c) = a\xd7b + a\xd7c. This is the link between multiplication and addition \u2014 very useful for mental arithmetic."},{term:"Absolute Value",meaning:"The distance of an integer from zero on the number line, always non-negative. Written as |a|. Examples: |\u22125| = 5, |3| = 3, |0| = 0."},{term:"Number Line",meaning:"A visual representation of numbers on a straight line, with zero at the centre, positive integers to the right, and negative integers to the left. Used to visualise integer operations."}],worked_examples:[{example_number:1,type:"Computation",question:"Evaluate: (\u22128) + 3 + (\u22125) + 12 + (\u22122)",step_by_step_answer:["Step 1: Group positive and negative integers separately.","Positives: 3 + 12 = 15.","Negatives: (\u22128) + (\u22125) + (\u22122) = \u221215.","Step 2: Add results: 15 + (\u221215) = 0.","Answer: 0.","Alternative: Use the associative and commutative properties to rearrange: [(\u22128) + 8] + [3 + (\u22123)] + [(\u22125) + 5] \u2014 but here we just group and add."]},{example_number:2,type:"Computation",question:"Evaluate: (\u22124) \xd7 (\u22123) \xd7 (\u22122) \xd7 5",step_by_step_answer:["Step 1: Count the number of negative integers \u2014 there are 3 (odd number). An odd count of negatives gives a NEGATIVE product.","Step 2: Multiply the absolute values: 4 \xd7 3 \xd7 2 \xd7 5 = 120.","Step 3: Apply the sign: 3 negatives (odd) \u2192 final answer is NEGATIVE.","Answer: \u2212120.","Verify: (\u22124) \xd7 (\u22123) = +12. 12 \xd7 (\u22122) = \u221224. (\u221224) \xd7 5 = \u2212120. \u2713"]},{example_number:3,type:"Word Problem",question:"In a quiz, each correct answer gives 5 marks and each wrong answer gives \u22122 marks. Rohan gets 8 correct and 5 wrong. What is his total score?",step_by_step_answer:["Step 1: Marks for correct answers = 8 \xd7 5 = 40.","Step 2: Marks for wrong answers = 5 \xd7 (\u22122) = \u221210.","Step 3: Total score = 40 + (\u221210) = 30.","Answer: Rohan's total score is 30 marks.","Extension: If Rohan needed 35 to pass, he fell short by 35 \u2212 30 = 5 marks."]},{example_number:4,type:"Property Application",question:"Verify the distributive property: (\u22123) \xd7 [(\u22124) + (7)]",step_by_step_answer:["Method 1 (Bracket first): (\u22124) + 7 = 3. Then (\u22123) \xd7 3 = \u22129.","Method 2 (Distribute): (\u22123) \xd7 (\u22124) + (\u22123) \xd7 7 = 12 + (\u221221) = 12 \u2212 21 = \u22129.","Both methods give \u22129. \u2713 The distributive property holds.","Distributive property: a \xd7 (b + c) = a\xd7b + a\xd7c. Here a = \u22123, b = \u22124, c = 7."]}],quick_check_questions:[{after_concept:"Integers \u2014 All Operations",questions:[{type:"Recall",q:"What is the additive inverse of \u22129?",a:"9. Because \u22129 + 9 = 0."},{type:"Recall",q:"State the sign rule: (\u2212) \xd7 (\u2212) = ?",a:"Positive (+). Negative times negative = positive."},{type:"Computation",q:"Evaluate: (\u22126) \xd7 (\u22124) \xf7 (\u22123)",a:"(\u22126) \xd7 (\u22124) = 24. 24 \xf7 (\u22123) = \u22128. Answer: \u22128."},{type:"Understanding",q:"Why are integers NOT closed under division?",a:"Because dividing two integers does not always give an integer. Example: (\u22127) \xf7 2 = \u22123.5, which is not an integer."},{type:"Application",q:"Temperature was \u22125\xb0C at night and rose 12\xb0C by noon. What is the noon temperature?",a:"\u22125 + 12 = 7\xb0C."}]}],key_takeaways:["Integers include all positive whole numbers, negative whole numbers, and zero: {\u2026, \u22122, \u22121, 0, 1, 2, \u2026}","Subtracting an integer = adding its additive inverse: a \u2212 b = a + (\u2212b).","Addition properties: Closure, Commutative, Associative, Additive Identity (0), Additive Inverse.","Multiplication sign rules: Same signs \u2192 Positive; Different signs \u2192 Negative.","Multiplication properties: Closure, Commutative, Associative, Distributive, Multiplicative Identity (1), Zero property.","Division sign rules: same as multiplication. Division by zero is UNDEFINED.","Integers are NOT closed under division (result may not be an integer).","An even number of negative factors in a product \u2192 Positive; Odd number \u2192 Negative.","Distributive property: a \xd7 (b + c) = a\xd7b + a\xd7c \u2014 very useful for simplification.","Real-life integers: temperatures, sea levels, profit/loss, altitudes, floors below ground."],mind_map:{root:"Integers",branches:[{branch:"Definition",sub:["All whole numbers: positive, negative, zero","Number line: negative \u2190 0 \u2192 positive","Absolute value |a| = distance from 0"]},{branch:"Addition & Subtraction",sub:["Number line movement","a \u2212 b = a + (\u2212b)","Properties: Closure, Commutative, Associative","Additive Identity: 0","Additive Inverse: a + (\u2212a) = 0"]},{branch:"Multiplication",sub:["(+)(+) = +, (\u2212)(\u2212) = +","(+)(\u2212) = \u2212, (\u2212)(+) = \u2212","Even negatives \u2192 +, Odd negatives \u2192 \u2212","Properties: Closure, Commutative, Associative, Distributive","Identity: \xd71 = itself, Zero: \xd70 = 0"]},{branch:"Division",sub:["Same sign rules as multiplication","(+)\xf7(+)=+, (\u2212)\xf7(\u2212)=+","(+)\xf7(\u2212)=\u2212, (\u2212)\xf7(+)=\u2212","Division by 0 = UNDEFINED","NOT closed under division"]},{branch:"Real-Life Uses",sub:["Temperature (\xb0C below zero)","Altitude / Sea depth","Profit (+) and Loss (\u2212)","Quiz scores, Bank balance"]}]},revision_notes:"Chapter 1 \u2014 Integers. Definition: {\u2026, \u22122, \u22121, 0, 1, 2, \u2026}. Subtraction: a \u2212 b = a + (\u2212b). Addition properties: Closure (sum is integer), Commutative (a+b=b+a), Associative ((a+b)+c=a+(b+c)), Identity (a+0=a), Inverse (a+(\u2212a)=0). Multiplication sign rules: same signs\u2192+, different signs\u2192\u2212. Even number of negatives\u2192+, odd\u2192\u2212. Multiplication properties: Closure, Commutative, Associative, Distributive (a\xd7(b+c)=ab+ac), Identity (\xd71), Zero (\xd70=0). Division: same sign rules as multiplication. Division by 0 = UNDEFINED. 0\xf7n=0 (n\u22600). Integers NOT closed under division. Key worked examples: grouping integers for addition; counting negatives for multiplication sign; word problems with scores/temperature/money.",must_remember_facts:["a \u2212 b = a + (\u2212b): subtraction is adding the additive inverse.","Additive inverse of a = \u2212a. Additive identity = 0.","Multiplicative identity = 1. a \xd7 0 = 0 for all integers.","Sign rules \u2014 Multiplication/Division: Same signs \u2192 +. Different signs \u2192 \u2212.","Even count of negative factors \u2192 positive product. Odd count \u2192 negative product.","Distributive: a \xd7 (b + c) = ab + ac.","Division by zero is UNDEFINED \u2014 never allowed.","Integers are closed under +, \u2212, \xd7 but NOT \xf7.","Commutative holds for + and \xd7; NOT for \u2212 and \xf7.","Associative holds for + and \xd7; NOT for \u2212 and \xf7."]}},2585,[]);